<Project>

  <!-- Replaces xunit.v3's build guard (_XunitValidateBuild in xunit.v3.core.targets),
       which refuses to build a .NET test project unless it emits a native apphost.
       Both test projects here deliberately set UseAppHost=false, so what is kept below
       is the half of that guard which still applies.

       Why the apphost has to go. The SDK stamps it out of a prebuilt template, and for
       net8.0 — the framework the gating tests must execute on (ADR-0022) — that template
       is restored from NuGet as Microsoft.NETCore.App.Host.&lt;rid&gt;, carrying the standard
       ELF interpreter /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2. So it starts under the host loader and
       binds the distribution's libc, then dlopens libcoreclr.so out of the nix runtime,
       which drags in nix glibc: two glibcs in one process. Where the host glibc is the
       older of the two the load fails outright — "version `GLIBC_ABI_DT_X86_64_PLT' not
       found" — which is how this reads on WSL2 Ubuntu. nixpkgs patches the apphost
       template it ships to the nix interpreter, but it ships only the one matching its
       own SDK; nothing patches the net8 template NuGet hands out. These two projects are
       the only net8 executables here, so they are the only things that hit it — the
       DemoHost is a Blazor WebAssembly project and emits no apphost at all.

       (Written while the tests targeted net8.0. Since ADR-0022 moved them to net10.0 the
       apphost would come from the SDK's own template, which nixpkgs does patch; the
       override stays until that is seen to work under nix on every platform, since the
       failure only ever showed on WSL2.)

       With no apphost, `dotnet test` runs the test dll through the nix muxer, which
       already carries the nix interpreter: one glibc throughout, and the same path taken
       on every platform rather than a Linux-only patch.

       What this gives up. xunit's stated reason for the guard is xunit.v3.runner.utility,
       which launches the executable; runners built on it, including some IDE test
       explorers, will not find one. `dotnet test` does not use it, and `dotnet test` is
       what the Definition of Done gates on.

       Why this file exists at all: MSBuild imports a package's buildTransitive targets
       after the project body, so the same override written in either .csproj would lose.
       It sits in tests/ so it reaches only the two test projects. -->
  <Target Name="_XunitValidateBuild" BeforeTargets="BeforeBuild" Condition=" '$(TargetFramework)' != '' ">
    <Error Text="xUnit.net v3 test projects must be executable (set project property '&lt;OutputType&gt;Exe&lt;/OutputType&gt;')."
           Condition=" '$(OutputType)' != 'Exe' " />
  </Target>

</Project>
